### Research notes and references used

I searched a focused set of official sources, engineering papers, and research reviews relevant to the four videos. No search can honestly guarantee that it has covered every paper ever published on all these topics.

1\. OpenAI, mathematical proof and CFD

OpenAI's announcement dated 8 September 2026 describes its claimed Navier–Stokes finite-time blow-up result and provides the written argument and Lean formalisation. Cite refs=turn450166view0 />

Bastounis, Circelli and Hansen, Navier–Stokes lost in translation: Why Lean verification of AI autoformalisation does not guarantee correct natural language proofs, arXiv preprint posted 6 October 2026. The authors raise a concern about the correspondence between the formalisation and written argument, while explicitly cautioning that their paper is about the translation issue rather than claiming to settle every question of the written argument's correctness. Cite refs=turn978727view1 />

The official Clay Mathematics Institute page provides the original background to the existence-and-smoothness problem. Cite refs=turn978727view0 />

2\. Turbulence models and butterfly valves

Ansys' CFD (Fluent and CFX) Turbulence Modeling training describes the importance of selecting a model for the application and required accuracy. Cite refs=turn294259search0 />

Del Toro and Johnson, Computational Fluid Dynamics Investigation of Butterfly Valve Performance Factors (2015), compared simulated performance factors with experimental data for various valve openings and flow conditions. Cite refs=turn256032search7 />

Numerical simulation and experimental validation of flow characteristics for a butterfly check valve in small modular reactor examined several turbulence models and compared numerical results with experiments. It found standard k–ε performed better within that study's configuration; it does not establish that k–ε is always best for other valves. Cite refs=turn644399search5 />

3\. Tesla turbine

Galindo and colleagues, Effect of disc spacing and pressure flow on a modifiable Tesla turbine: Experimental and numerical analysis (2021), investigated prototype geometry, operating pressure, disk spacing, torque and CFD. The work supports modelling the actual disk and casing geometry and comparing predictions with experimental measurements. Cite refs=turn256032search1 />

A 2026 review of Tesla turbines in organic Rankine and transcritical CO₂ cycles highlights design, sealing, material, structural and high-pressure experimental-validation challenges. Those results apply to the investigated turbine configurations, not all Tesla turbines universally. Cite refs=turn429569search3 />

4\. Medical and aeronautical CFD

Skripka and colleagues, Numerical Aspects of Turbulent Blood Flow in Aorta: A Systematic Review (2026), reviews 38 studies and notes that turbulence modelling, boundary conditions and validation methods still lack a single unified standard. Cite refs=turn256032search0 />

The review Computational fluid dynamics modelling of hemodynamics in aortic aneurysm and dissection explains why pulsatility and the Womersley number matter alongside Reynolds number in aortic-flow modelling. Cite refs=turn256032search3 />

Mani and Dorgan, A Perspective on the State of Aerospace Computational Fluid Dynamics Technology (2023), reviews geometry, turbulence models, numerical algorithms, mesh adaptation and design optimisation as important aspects of aerospace CFD. Cite refs=turn644399search6 />

The central scientific message across all four videos is the same: a mathematical result can inspire research, CFD can test a specified model, and experiments plus rigorous validation help determine whether an engineering prediction is dependable.
